Language: en

00:00:00.910 --> 00:00:02.950 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  months of protest  in belarus an armed battle between  azerbaijan and armenia  a violent uprising after a disputed  parliamentary election in kyrgyzstan  tensions in these former soviet  republics have mounted despite vladimir  putin's efforts to keep control and  bring back stability  weakened by the economic impact of the  pandemic moscow has fewer resources to  spend on the upkeep of military outposts  in countries like armenia or kyrgyzstan  and its power in the region is waning  russia's  dominant position is no longer really  dominant  and it's increasingly challenged by  regional powers moscow is spending  on military forces troops in the regions  that it cares about  like in armenia azerbaijan kyrgyzstan  countries of the former soviet union but  right now what's priority for russia  is ensuring that it pumps money  into its economy in order to keep people  satisfied  as competitors like china and turkey are  expanding their presence in the region  30 years after the end of the soviet  union russia's monopoly on power in its  former territories  is under threat  russia has maintained strong  relationships with former soviet  republics  and even pushed to deepen economic and  military ties in recent years  belarus kyrgyzstan and armenia have been  part of the eurasian economic union  single market since 2015  and russian forces are also stationed in  belarus armenia and kyrgyzstan  but other countries have also invested  money and increased military cooperation  in that region  turkey for example has thrown its  military support behind azerbaijan in  the conflict with armenia  a close russian ally in the disputed  region of nagorno-karabakh  russia has good relations both with  armenia and azerbaijan  and actually sells weapons to both of  these countries  and it's a way of balancing the  influence in the region  wary of taking sides russia wants to  play a role as mediator in the conflict  and helped armenia and azerbaijan reach  a temporary ceasefire  but fighting is continued and both sides  are accusing each other of violating the  truce agreements  highlighting the limits of russia's  power turkey's involvement is putting  pressure on russia to protect its  armenian ally  but analysts say moscow wants to avoid a  broader conflict  and russia doesn't want to get involved  because it doesn't have too many  resources  in kyrgyzstan clashes between political  factions have been driven by local  conflicts  and claims of rigged elections beyond  calling it chaos  the kremlin hasn't officially supported  either party  russia has taken a very kind of more  relaxed  hands-off approach to the conflict in  that region  the pro-russian president resigned days  after meeting with his nationalist  opponent with the mediation of putin's  senior aides  but in this country where russia has a  military base  another superpower has expanded its  presence in recent years  china shares a border with kyrgyzstan  and has invested heavily in  infrastructure as part of its initiative  known as belt and road  russia is very aware of the kind of  influence that beijing could have in  central asia and in a country like  kyrgyzstan  russia can take this laid-back approach  but it realizes that it still needs to  show that it cares  about kyrgyzstan because the instant it  shows that it does not care china could  easily move in and have more of an  influence there than russia would care  for it to have  in belarus it's a different story  protests have continued for months  but that doesn't mean russia isn't in  control  [Music]  president alexander lukashenko is  holding on to power despite accusations  of a rigged election  as pressure has mounted on his  presidency he's accepted russia's  demands to discuss new military bases  and trade agreements  even as belarus already relies heavily  on russia for crude  in oil short to medium term russia is  actually winning  because with the regime be deeply  wounded  it looks like belarus moving much closer  towards  russia on the other hand moscow has  avoided expressing clear support for  lukashenko  suggesting that it doesn't want to  estrange belarusians  some of whom have called for the  european union to intervene  the kremlin is very conscious of that by  siding  with mr lukashenko russia actually risks  alienating the biela russian people and  that's a huge risk for russia  so rise of regional powers and the lure  of european unions west  china in the east and then turkey and  south caucasus is really  a daunting challenge for russia after  two decades of aggressive military  campaigns  in countries like georgia and ukraine  putin's approach to foreign policy in  russia's backyard has shifted  as he prepares to potentially remain in  power until 2036  the russian president is himself under  pressure to revitalize his country's  waning economy while fighting the  pandemic  president putin has seen protests in the  far east  people are dissatisfied people have lost  their jobs because of coronavirus and he  needs to show his population  that the priority is here at home and  the priority  isn't pumping money into foreign  escapades

